course: dictionary
lesson: 3423
title: External
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: External means on the outside, or coming from outside. The key idea is outside. «A backup is an extra copy kept somewhere else, such as a cloud service or an external drive.» The words somewhere else are the clue: an external drive is a separate storage device outside the computer, not something hidden inside it.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Such as a cloud service or an outside drive» still makes sense, so external is on the right track. The same idea works in other sentences: an external wall is on the outside of a building, and an external examiner comes from outside your own school.
fact: External means on the outside, or coming from outside.
fact: «External» can mean coming from another place or person, as in «an external examiner».
fact: «For external use only» on a medicine label means do not swallow it. It is for the outside of the body.
fact: In maths, an external angle is an angle outside a shape.
fact: As a noun, «externals» means the outward appearance of something or someone.
exercise: «A backup is an extra copy kept somewhere else, such as a cloud service or an external drive.» What does external mean in this sentence? | Made of wood | *Outside the computer, a separate drive you plug in | Inside the computer, hidden in the case | Broken and no longer working
exercise: A medicine label says «For external use only». What does this mean? | Take it with a big glass of water | *Do not swallow it; use it on the outside of the body | Only for children | Keep it in the fridge
exercise: A school asks for an «external examiner» to mark the test. Which is true? | The examiner is the class teacher | The examiner is a pupil from the same class | The examiner is a type of pencil | *The examiner comes from outside the school
test: Which word describes the outside of a building in «external walls»? | The roof tiles | The furniture | *The outside of the building | The inside rooms
test: What is an «external factor»? | *Something from outside a situation that affects it | A feeling inside a person's mind | A sum in maths | A type of weather
exam: Which phrase means an examiner from outside your own school? | An examiner's desk | A school uniform | *An external examiner | An internal examiner
